Z||000000||ounmongk||05-25-2000||12:10 AM||||hi again.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I've made up my mind: gonna forget about performance and go into sound...  I saw in an old issue of AS&S that featured a stealth box setup in an Audi A4. I didn't really care about the subwoofer setup, but moreso about the way that the line driver was mounted. it was put IN the rear of the driver's seat. Meaning that the back of the driver's seat was "gutted" and line driver was put there. (not too sure what a line driver is) anyways, is this a good idea? would the amp get enough air to cool itself? because I think I'm going to skimp on the warrantee this time around.  <BR>Before the Fiero, I had a chevy astro van that I put three 8" MTX Terminators in. those were the days: lots of room, lots of air. &lt;tear rolls down my eye&gt; thanks in advance, everyone. <BR>Ohm. <P>P.S. sorry bout the long a$$ post.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||olympic||05-25-2000||02:52 AM||||  The amp would probably be fine back there as long as the cooling fins stay exposed and are no pressed up against anything when the seat is in its normal position.  If not done carefully or if the amp is too thick, your seat may become hard(from the loss of padding) in the spot where the amp is.  Oh well, when the amp heats up you'll have heated seats.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>   BTW, a line driver takes the low level(RCA) signal from your cd player and boosts it up to 4-10 volts or higher and feeds that into the amps or eq.  Therefore you can turn the gain on your amps way down reducing the noise floor annd any induced noise.||||reg||1
Z||000002||dragnlo||05-25-2000||10:17 AM||||I do not know how much experience you have or your skill level, but it is a major undertaking to mount things in the back of the seat and make it look good.  I have done it(not in my car) and seen it done many other cars. Things to plan on:<BR><OL TYPE=1><BR><LI> attaching some frame to the seat to mount the amp to<BR><LI>remove the foam without screwing up the seat is a chore within its self<BR><LI>like olympic said cut out to much foam prepare to sit on a hard amp(not fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR><LI>running wires thru the seat (can pinch wires with the seat if it has tilt)<BR><LI>upholstery what i have found that works fairly good is cut out 1/4" masonite to the width of the seatand then cut a viewing hole for the amp, this gives you a surface to fold the upholstery over onto<BR></OL><BR>I dont mean to discourage you and it can look very cool. I just hate when people try things and they dont fully realize what its going to take(aka take the seat out of the car and then cant drive it for 2 weeks, thinking they will have it done in a day)<BR>I hope this helps<BR>dragnlo||||reg||1
Z||000003||ounmongk||05-26-2000||02:16 AM||||I know how hard it's going to be... I think this'll all go on the pass. side. I think it would realy really save space on this install. thanks again||||reg||1
